      "title": "The Corner Library",
      "text": "For years, an empty lot stood beside the Rahman family's apartment building. It collected broken bottles, old boxes, and rainwater. Mrs Rahman often said that the space made the street feel forgotten. One spring, her son Sami suggested building a small library there. The family asked their neighbours for help.\n\nAt first, some people laughed. They thought books would get wet or disappear. However, Mr Rahman found two strong wooden cabinets at a repair shop. Neighbours painted them bright blue and added glass doors. Sami's aunt gave them a waterproof roof, while children made signs that said, “Take a book, leave a book.”\n\nThe library opened on a sunny Saturday in June. It began with 86 books, including storybooks, cookbooks, and books for learning languages. A local café placed two chairs beside it. Within a month, people were stopping there on their way home. Some exchanged books, and others simply talked about what they were reading.\n\nThe lot did not become perfect overnight. The family still cleans it every Friday, and a neighbour checks the doors after heavy rain. Yet Mrs Rahman now calls it the friendliest corner of the street. She says the project gave people a reason to notice one another.",
      "table": null
    },
